KG42.register("nodes-1ab4123ad1d8e22895b6e52e",{"id":"COMP-166b347ab5fcd2a9","code":"PRC001","label":"Bilateral price adjustment, exogenous network-tariff publication and revision, and convergence stopping","definition":"The bilateral transaction price is updated according to λ_nm^{k+1}=λ_nm^k−ρ(p_nm^{k+1}+p_mn^{k+1})/2. The system operator announces exogenous network charges ex ante and may revise the unit rate according to network conditions or historical revenue adequacy and congestion conditions. The unit also independently contains residual broadcasting and global primal and dual stopping procedures.","layer":"L6","template_code":"BCT","concept_id":"coordination.price_signal","concept_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"identity_dimensions":{"identity_fields":["Object: the bilateral transaction price, the exogenous network-charge signal, and the negotiation convergence criterion","Core operation: updating the transaction price according to quantity inconsistency between the two parties; announcing and revising the rate; aggregating residuals for the stopping decision","Output: the updated price and charge signals, and the judgment to continue negotiation or to stop"]},"exclusions":["The transaction price is updated after counterproposals from all partners have been received, ρ>0","The grid features of electrical distance and zoning policy are assumed not to change with time; the unit rate can adapt to daytime and nighttime network conditions","Periodic revision is based on the previous period's revenue adequacy and congestion incidence; annual revision is only an example","The residual test and the global primal and dual tolerance tests are retained respectively according to source equations (7)–(8); stopping occurs only when both criteria are satisfied","The price entering local optimization, the clearing objective, and partner selection belong to the use context of the signal"],"members":[{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"The trading partnership set restricts demand proposals: the bilateral quantity must be zero for a counterparty outside that set. Scope: Buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. Condition: When a prospective counterparty is outside the requesting agent's trading partnership set. Current bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its requested energy quantities. Scope: Buying-side quantity updates in the decentralized consensus-ADMM negotiation of (6). Condition: At each negotiation iteration. Participants update requested quantities through their own optimization (6a) and individually send the resulting trade proposals to their trading partners; the negotiation process repeats until convergence. Scope: Submission of buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. Condition: During each iteration of the negotiation process, until convergence. Requested quantities must be updated consistently with the agent's total-power bounds, with total power equal to the sum of its bilateral proposals. Scope: Requested-power updates in (6a), jointly with the agent's other bilateral proposals. Condition: At every negotiation iteration. Each prosumer's requested bilateral quantity must remain within that prosumer's power boundaries. Scope: Individual buying-side bilateral proposals in the update (6a). Condition: When the requesting agent is a prosumer. The prescribed update prohibits buying requests from generators by constraining their bilateral proposed quantities to be nonnegative. Scope: Bilateral quantity proposals produced by the update (6a). Condition: When the updating agent is a generator.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"Bilateral quantity proposals produced by the update (6a). | Buying-side quantity updates in the decentralized consensus-ADMM negotiation of (6). | Buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. | Individual buying-side bilateral proposals in the update (6a). | Requested-power updates in (6a), jointly with the agent's other bilateral proposals. | Submission of buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002"],"decision_anchor":"bidding.demand","source_rule_sha256":"dd9010023e8d5e8a6fc38f03180aa098dab283f108a8e7f7d06a7719cdb0d88c","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001","node_id":"coordination.price_signal","function":"coordination.price_signal","unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::coordination.price_signal","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"The partner's previous requested quantity enters the averaging and penalty terms used to update the offered quantity. Scope: Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). Condition: The partner counterproposal represents requested power. The previous bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its offered quantities, economically encouraging consensus with its partners. Scope: Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). The trading partnership set restricts possible supply offers: the bilateral quantity to a counterparty outside that set must be zero. Scope: Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n's trading partnership set. The participant sends its updated offered quantities to the corresponding trading partners. Scope: Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration. Condition: After the participant has updated its trade proposals through its own optimization (6a). When updating offered quantities, the participant's total power, summed across all its bilateral proposals, must remain within its lower and upper power bounds. Scope: Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. Each bilateral offered quantity must also lie within the prosumer's individual power boundaries. Scope: Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the prosumer set. Consumers cannot submit positive selling quantities: their bilateral proposal quantities must be nonpositive. Scope: Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the consumer set.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). | Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). | Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). | Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). | Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. | Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. | Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002"],"decision_anchor":"bidding.supply","source_rule_sha256":"eb3e04d94a468918abeb09de0a73b5857fe82afe93cb43afba9b3825477aa259","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002","node_id":"coordination.price_signal","function":"coordination.price_signal","unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::coordination.price_signal","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"Network-charge price incentives, adjusted through unit fees, influence the aggregate-cost-minimizing P2P economic dispatch. Scope: Deterministic, single-time-step exogenous P2P economic dispatch (5). Condition: When the proposed exogenous network-charge policies are applied. The reference clearing objective minimizes the sum of agents' cost functions. Scope: Endogenous, network-constrained P2P economic-dispatch reference formulation (1). Condition: For the reference formulation before replacement of network constraints by exogenous cost allocation.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Deterministic, single-time-step exogenous P2P economic dispatch (5). | Endogenous, network-constrained P2P economic-dispatch reference formulation (1).","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e07","v141-b-g01-e14","v141-b-g03-e11","v141-b-g03-e15"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.social_welfare","source_rule_sha256":"739c152a19529f8dbc08a6ecf72068fc6345650543ebce4f413fa8a328e86477","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003","node_id":"coordination.price_signal","function":"coordination.price_signal","unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::coordination.price_signal","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","market_meaning":"An offered energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals offering energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals. A requested energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals requesting energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals. Network-charge signals are provided transparently before negotiations so participants can anticipate additional costs of using the power system. Scope: Exogenous network-charge signals in the P2P negotiation mechanism. Condition: When exogenous terms are used as network charges. Unit-fee signals may be adapted to grid status, including differences between day and night. Scope: Electrical-distance and zonal network-charge policies, whose underlying grid characteristics are assumed time independent. The system operator may update unit-fee signals based on the previous period's revenue adequacy and congestion occurrence rate; yearly revision is an example, not a mandatory frequency. Scope: Periodic revision of network-charge unit-fee signals under the proposed cost-allocation policies. Condition: When the system operator periodically revises unit fees using historical information from the previous period. Each agent broadcasts its local residuals and tests both global primal and dual stopping criteria; the decentralized negotiation repeats until convergence. Scope: Convergence checks in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: At each iteration, the global stopping test follows receipt of all other agents' local residuals.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). | Convergence checks in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). | Electrical-distance and zonal network-charge policies, whose underlying grid characteristics are assumed time independent. | Exogenous network-charge signals in the P2P negotiation mechanism. | Periodic revision of network-charge unit-fee signals under the proposed cost-allocation policies.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e16","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e19","v141-b-g01-e20","v141-b-g02-E14"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C005","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C006"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.price_signal","source_rule_sha256":"76fd9752e5725e328e480f37d56f7add84aa4d6520433ed0697820cc4a9816e5","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004","node_id":"coordination.price_signal","function":"coordination.price_signal","unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::coordination.price_signal","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"The electrical-distance network-charge signal is intended to encourage agents to trade with their electrically closest partners. Scope: Proposed electrical-distance cost allocation in the P2P community. Condition: Network charges are proportional to the electrical distance between agents. A high zonal price is intended to discourage outside agents from trading with counterparties inside that zone. Scope: Proposed zonal cost allocation with distinct zonal unit fees. Condition: A zone has a high price. Network-charge signals can induce agents to switch from their usual partners to partners unaffected by the malfunctions while retaining the same negotiation routine. Scope: Proposed use of new unit fees to handle deteriorated network operation in the P2P market. Condition: An electric-network incident causes malfunctions affecting trading partners. The bilateral trade power from agent n to agent m must be zero when m is outside n’s partnership set. Scope: Bilateral trading partnerships in the P2P formulation. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n’s trading partnership set. Partnership sets specify possible trades and do not themselves enforce market participation. Scope: Role of partnership sets in the exogenous P2P formulation. Partnership sets could be dynamically adapted in response to congestion; this is a proposed possibility rather than an asserted implemented procedure. Scope: Discussed alternative for handling congestion through changes to partnership sets. Condition: Congestion occurs.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Bilateral trading partnerships in the P2P formulation. | Discussed alternative for handling congestion through changes to partnership sets. | Proposed electrical-distance cost allocation in the P2P community. | Proposed use of new unit fees to handle deteriorated network operation in the P2P market. | Proposed zonal cost allocation with distinct zonal unit fees. | Role of partnership sets in the exogenous P2P formulation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e15","v141-b-g01-e23","v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E02","v141-b-g02-E06","v141-b-g02-E11"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003"],"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","source_rule_sha256":"17c2f49b0636db1a4fd2c1e6da9d2ec09881219b8198137ad05850f17c990419","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005","node_id":"coordination.price_signal","function":"coordination.price_signal","unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::coordination.price_signal","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","anchor":false}],"paper_ids":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"a competitive equilibrium which efﬁciency strongly depends on w the chosen network charges. Accordin","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b).","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","quote":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"a competitive equilibrium which efﬁciency strongly depends on w the chosen network charges. Accordin","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b).","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-validation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 4, JULY 2019 fn to be closed, proper, and convex is","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"where penalty factor \\rho>0. Element \\lambda_{nm} of matrix \\Lambda corresponds to generation price of electricity for traded volume p_{nm}. Possible trades of agents n can be grouped in variable P_n=(p_{nm})_{m\\in\\omega_n}. According to [11], supposing cost functions f_n to be closed, proper, and convex is a sufficient condition to ensure convergence of (6). This formulation allows to have primal feasibility of constraints (5c)–(5g) at each iteration step. However, primal feasibility of trades reciprocity (5b) is only verified at the limit after convergence. Note that the additional terms of the augmented Lagrangian in (6a) aims at encouraging, economically, an agent n to reach power consensus with its partners.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"where penalty factor \\rho>0. Element \\lambda_{nm} of matrix \\Lambda corresponds to generation price of electricity for traded volume p_{nm}. Possible trades of agents n can be grouped in variable P_n=(p_{nm})_{m\\in\\omega_n}. According to [11], supposing cost functions f_n to be closed, proper, and convex is a sufficient condition to ensure convergence of (6). This formulation allows to have primal feasibility of constraints (5c)–(5g) at each iteration step. However, primal feasibility of trades reciprocity (5b) is only verified at the limit after convergence. Note that the additional terms of the augmented Lagrangian in (6a) aims at encouraging, economically, an agent n to reach power consensus with its partners."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"for its use of the power system. In problem (3) with (4), reciprocity constraint (3b) is the of only","equation_id":"Eq. (5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In problem (3) with (4), reciprocity constraint (3b) is the only barrier to fully distribute it. To overcome this an additional slack variable W can be considered. Variable W, which can contribute to reach consensus, aims at being the image of all possible trades P. For this, reciprocity constraint (3b) is replaced by power consensus constraint (5b) leading to the deterministic, single time-step, exogenous P2P economic dispatch \\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega,W}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left[f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right] (5a); s.t. (W-W^{T})/2=P (5b); p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}, n\\in\\Omega (5c); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega (5d); p_{nm}\\ge0, n\\in\\Omega_g (5e); p_{nm}\\le0, n\\in\\Omega_c (5f); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega_p. (5g).","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"In problem (3) with (4), reciprocity constraint (3b) is the only barrier to fully distribute it. To overcome this an additional slack variable W can be considered. Variable W, which can contribute to reach consensus, aims at being the image of all possible trades P. For this, reciprocity constraint (3b) is replaced by power consensus constraint (5b) leading to the deterministic, single time-step, exogenous P2P economic dispatch \\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega,W}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left[f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right] (5a); s.t. (W-W^{T})/2=P (5b); p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}, n\\in\\Omega (5c); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega (5d); p_{nm}\\ge0, n\\in\\Omega_g (5e); p_{nm}\\le0, n\\in\\Omega_c (5f); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega_p. (5g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p09-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Case Studies","anchor_text":"p y , y q allocation, electrical distance and zonal cost allocations enable ma to isolate zones econ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Despite these results, market aggregation by zone does not reflect how the electrical grid is used. Cost allocation policies provides a tool for the system operator to affect the economic dispatch. Unit fees enable to tweak the expected outcomes of the P2P market in such way that line capacities might not be violated. They also offer to the system operator the chance to look for cost recovery.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Despite these results, market aggregation by zone does not reflect how the electrical grid is used. Cost allocation policies provides a tool for the system operator to affect the economic dispatch. Unit fees enable to tweak the expected outcomes of the P2P market in such way that line capacities might not be violated. They also offer to the system operator the chance to look for cost recovery."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e15","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p10-c2r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Case Studies","anchor_text":"nomic dispatch, the proposed method based on network charges brings transparency and is simple to im","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Peer-to-peer markets are considered as a likely evolution of the power systems driven by distributed energy resources and ICT development. In this paper a peer-to-peer electricity market including network charges has been considered. Network charges, provided a priori, have been used as incentives to account for grid-related costs in a simple and transparent way. This mechanism incites market participants to respect power system’s limits, rather than enforcing them. Tested for three incentive frameworks, on a novel test case based on the IEEE 39-bus test system, it has been shown the ability of this mechanism to limit the stress put on the physical grid by the market.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Peer-to-peer markets are considered as a likely evolution of the power systems driven by distributed energy resources and ICT development. In this paper a peer-to-peer electricity market including network charges has been considered. Network charges, provided a priori, have been used as incentives to account for grid-related costs in a simple and transparent way. This mechanism incites market participants to respect power system’s limits, rather than enforcing them. Tested for three incentive frameworks, on a novel test case based on the IEEE 39-bus test system, it has been shown the ability of this mechanism to limit the stress put on the physical grid by the market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e13","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"t s pu pose. e , egu a at o u ct o h eva uat g et 2) work constraints is be replaced by a cost alloc","equation_id":"Eq. (4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"However, even though (3) enables the system operator to recover congestion related costs, it does not guarantee the recovery of the costs of maintenance, modernization of power lines, taxes, and policies. This paper proposes to replace the regularization function h with exogenous terms. These exogenous terms would aim not only at allocating congestion-related costs but also costs of maintenance and modernization of power lines, taxes, and policies such as e.g. renewable support schemes. Preference prices as introduced in [18] seems a good candidate for this purpose. Then, regularization function h evaluating network constraints is be replaced by a cost allocation function defined as \\Gamma(\\mathbf{P})=\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left(\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right) (4); where parameter \\gamma_{nm} is the network charge associated to power trade p_{nm} for the given time step. Constant terms \\gamma_n^0, which do not affect the minimization outcome, allow reflecting costs that are independent of the power traded, such as power line investment and maintenance. Network charges \\gamma_{nm}, detailed in Section III, would then account for congestion-related costs and taxes. Function \\Gamma is separable among participants, and will be integrated in their objective function as it will be further discussed in Section III. Note that \\Gamma also represents the amount of money collected by the system operator from community \\Omega for its use of the power system.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"However, even though (3) enables the system operator to recover congestion related costs, it does not guarantee the recovery of the costs of maintenance, modernization of power lines, taxes, and policies. This paper proposes to replace the regularization function h with exogenous terms. These exogenous terms would aim not only at allocating congestion-related costs but also costs of maintenance and modernization of power lines, taxes, and policies such as e.g. renewable support schemes. Preference prices as introduced in [18] seems a good candidate for this purpose. Then, regularization function h evaluating network constraints is be replaced by a cost allocation function defined as \\Gamma(\\mathbf{P})=\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left(\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right) (4); where parameter \\gamma_{nm} is the network charge associated to power trade p_{nm} for the given time step. Constant terms \\gamma_n^0, which do not affect the minimization outcome, allow reflecting costs that are independent of the power traded, such as power line investment and maintenance. Network charges \\gamma_{nm}, detailed in Section III, would then account for congestion-related costs and taxes. Function \\Gamma is separable among participants, and will be integrated in their objective function as it will be further discussed in Section III. Note that \\Gamma also represents the amount of money collected by the system operator from community \\Omega for its use of the power system."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p01-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"p ( ), wi NOMENCLATURE sto to Λ Matrix of trade prices λnm of agent n with agent m con ·T Matrix tra","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Λ Matrix of trade prices λ_nm of agent n with agent m; \\cdot^{T} Matrix transpose operator; γ_nm Network charges of agent n’s trade with agent m; L Set of lines in the power system; N Set of nodes in the power system; | · | Absolute value operator; Ω Peer-to-peer market community of N_Ω agents; Ω_c Set of consumers (subset of Ω); Ω_g Set of generators (subset of Ω); Ω_p Set of prosumers (subset of Ω); ω_n Set of partners of agent n; P Matrix of trade powers p_nm of agent n with agent m; θ_i Voltage angle at node i; \\underline{p}_n/\\overline{p}_n Lower/upper power boundary of agent n; d_nm Electrical distance between agent n and agent m; f_n Cost function of agent n; N^{zone}_{nm} Number of zones between agent n and agent m; p_n Total amount of power traded by agent n; u Network fee of cost allocation policy; Z_il/Y_il Element of bus impedance/admittance matrix from node i to node l.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Λ Matrix of trade prices λ_nm of agent n with agent m; \\cdot^{T} Matrix transpose operator; γ_nm Network charges of agent n’s trade with agent m; L Set of lines in the power system; N Set of nodes in the power system; | · | Absolute value operator; Ω Peer-to-peer market community of N_Ω agents; Ω_c Set of consumers (subset of Ω); Ω_g Set of generators (subset of Ω); Ω_p Set of prosumers (subset of Ω); ω_n Set of partners of agent n; P Matrix of trade powers p_nm of agent n with agent m; θ_i Voltage angle at node i; \\underline{p}_n/\\overline{p}_n Lower/upper power boundary of agent n; d_nm Electrical distance between agent n and agent m; f_n Cost function of agent n; N^{zone}_{nm} Number of zones between agent n and agent m; p_n Total amount of power traded by agent n; u Network fee of cost allocation policy; Z_il/Y_il Element of bus impedance/admittance matrix from node i to node l."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (1e)-(1g)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In this situation agents can buy a large amount of energy at a low price from one agent to sell it back at a higher price to another. This possibility of arbitraging can be proscribed by limiting the possible amounts traded. Generators, for which p_n\\ge0 and grouped in \\Omega_g, are forbidden to buy power in (1e). On the other hand consumers, for which p_n\\le0 and grouped in \\Omega_c, are forbidden to sell power in (1f). However, prosumers, gathered in \\Omega_p, must still be able to either buy or sell power since they are such that \\underline{p}_n\u003c0\u003c\\overline{p}_n. Power trades of prosumers are bounded by their power boundaries as in (1g).","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"In this situation agents can buy a large amount of energy at a low price from one agent to sell it back at a higher price to another. This possibility of arbitraging can be proscribed by limiting the possible amounts traded. Generators, for which p_n\\ge0 and grouped in \\Omega_g, are forbidden to buy power in (1e). On the other hand consumers, for which p_n\\le0 and grouped in \\Omega_c, are forbidden to sell power in (1f). However, prosumers, gathered in \\Omega_p, must still be able to either buy or sell power since they are such that \\underline{p}_n\u003c0\u003c\\overline{p}_n. Power trades of prosumers are bounded by their power boundaries as in (1g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"a competitive equilibrium which efﬁciency strongly depends on w the chosen network charges. Accordin","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b).","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-validation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 4, JULY 2019 fn to be closed, proper, and convex is","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"where penalty factor \\rho>0. Element \\lambda_{nm} of matrix \\Lambda corresponds to generation price of electricity for traded volume p_{nm}. Possible trades of agents n can be grouped in variable P_n=(p_{nm})_{m\\in\\omega_n}. According to [11], supposing cost functions f_n to be closed, proper, and convex is a sufficient condition to ensure convergence of (6). This formulation allows to have primal feasibility of constraints (5c)–(5g) at each iteration step. However, primal feasibility of trades reciprocity (5b) is only verified at the limit after convergence. Note that the additional terms of the augmented Lagrangian in (6a) aims at encouraging, economically, an agent n to reach power consensus with its partners.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"where penalty factor \\rho>0. Element \\lambda_{nm} of matrix \\Lambda corresponds to generation price of electricity for traded volume p_{nm}. Possible trades of agents n can be grouped in variable P_n=(p_{nm})_{m\\in\\omega_n}. According to [11], supposing cost functions f_n to be closed, proper, and convex is a sufficient condition to ensure convergence of (6). This formulation allows to have primal feasibility of constraints (5c)–(5g) at each iteration step. However, primal feasibility of trades reciprocity (5b) is only verified at the limit after convergence. Note that the additional terms of the augmented Lagrangian in (6a) aims at encouraging, economically, an agent n to reach power consensus with its partners."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e16","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"Note that the deﬁnition of partnership set ωn only inform on agent n’s possible trades but does not ","equation_id":"Eq. (5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"As proven in Appendix A, when no network charges are applied, i.e. all \\gamma_{nm}=0, trading prices \\lambda_{nm} are uniform and equal to the pool market price. The system operator does not intervene in solving (5) as it only provides network charges \\gamma_{nm} a priori. Hence, network charges are provided in a transparent manner before negotiations such that agents can anticipate on the over costs brought by the use of the power system.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"As proven in Appendix A, when no network charges are applied, i.e. all \\gamma_{nm}=0, trading prices \\lambda_{nm} are uniform and equal to the pool market price. The system operator does not intervene in solving (5) as it only provides network charges \\gamma_{nm} a priori. Hence, network charges are provided in a transparent manner before negotiations such that agents can anticipate on the over costs brought by the use of the power system."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p02-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"gy g , pp y a network charge component. This network charge component is not only to recover all net","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In contrast, the bilateral contracts considered here have a component that is based on energy generation costs, supplemented by a network charge component. This network charge component is not only to recover all network costs but also other costs e.g. operational, taxes and policy-related costs. Hence, network constraints are not forced directly but rather accommodated through these network charges. Another important benefit of this approach is that market participants have knowledge of network charges prior to the negotiation process. Contrary to a classical economic dispatch, this transparency on network charges enables agents to anticipate on what it will cost them to trade on the network.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"In contrast, the bilateral contracts considered here have a component that is based on energy generation costs, supplemented by a network charge component. This network charge component is not only to recover all network costs but also other costs e.g. operational, taxes and policy-related costs. Hence, network constraints are not forced directly but rather accommodated through these network charges. Another important benefit of this approach is that market participants have knowledge of network charges prior to the negotiation process. Contrary to a classical economic dispatch, this transparency on network charges enables agents to anticipate on what it will cost them to trade on the network."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e21","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"local residuals. In this context it would be interesting to use a secured mechanism, as does [15] fo","equation_id":"Eq. (4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"When the goal is to obtain a P2P market with allocation of grid-related costs it is possible to use network charges as in (4). Contrary to preference prices chosen by agents, parameters \\gamma_{nm} are provided by the system operator a priori when used as network charges. As mentioned in Section I, remember that, in this paper, a cost allocation policy refers to the way costs are divided between P2P market participants. The proposed cost allocation policies will define how network charges are estimated.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"When the goal is to obtain a P2P market with allocation of grid-related costs it is possible to use network charges as in (4). Contrary to preference prices chosen by agents, parameters \\gamma_{nm} are provided by the system operator a priori when used as network charges. As mentioned in Section I, remember that, in this paper, a cost allocation policy refers to the way costs are divided between P2P market participants. The proposed cost allocation policies will define how network charges are estimated."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E14","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"operator can periodically, e.g. yearly, update unit fees based et- on the revenue adequacy and the c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Both electrical distance and zonal cost allocation policies depend on grid characteristics, supposed time independent. Their unit fees can be adapted to grid’s status (e.g. between day and night). As any exogenous approach the proposed allocation policies may not ensure efficiency of the P2P market, as pointed in [16] in the case of transmission rights. Even though local marginal prices seem effective, they may be largely rejected for their opacity by P2P market participants anxious for transparency. To define the unit fees, as well as zones, the system operator can periodically, e.g. yearly, update unit fees based on the revenue adequacy and the congestion occurrence rate of the last period. This type of historical data analysis is a common practice. For example the French transmission operator, RTE, publishes its transmission tariffs (or TURPE for Tarif d’Utilisation du Réseau Public d’Electricité) based on this type of analysis. More details on this method can be found in the “Study on tariff design for distribution systems” prepared for the European Commission. Alternatively, zones can follow administrative delimitation such as states.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Both electrical distance and zonal cost allocation policies depend on grid characteristics, supposed time independent. Their unit fees can be adapted to grid’s status (e.g. between day and night). As any exogenous approach the proposed allocation policies may not ensure efficiency of the P2P market, as pointed in [16] in the case of transmission rights. Even though local marginal prices seem effective, they may be largely rejected for their opacity by P2P market participants anxious for transparency. To define the unit fees, as well as zones, the system operator can periodically, e.g. yearly, update unit fees based on the revenue adequacy and the congestion occurrence rate of the last period. This type of historical data analysis is a common practice. For example the French transmission operator, RTE, publishes its transmission tariffs (or TURPE for Tarif d’Utilisation du Réseau Public d’Electricité) based on this type of analysis. More details on this method can be found in the “Study on tariff design for distribution systems” prepared for the European Commission. Alternatively, zones can follow administrative delimitation such as states."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e19","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-validation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"rm on. n∈Ω n∈Ω the with, respectively, primal and dual local residuals ies 2 rk+1n = pk+1nm + pk+1mn","equation_id":"Eq. (7)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Global stopping criteria associated to (6) are such as \\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left\\|r_n^{k+1}\\right\\|^2\\le\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{pri}2} and \\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left\\|s_n^{k+1}\\right\\|^2\\le\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{dual}2} (7); with, respectively, primal and dual local residuals r_n^{k+1}=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1}\\right)^2 (8a); s_n^{k+1}=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}-p_{nm}^{k}\\right)^2 (8b); Parameters \\epsilon^{\\mathrm{pri}} and \\epsilon^{\\mathrm{dual}} denotes primal and dual global feasibility tolerances, respectively.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Global stopping criteria associated to (6) are such as \\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left\\|r_n^{k+1}\\right\\|^2\\le\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{pri}2} and \\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left\\|s_n^{k+1}\\right\\|^2\\le\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{dual}2} (7); with, respectively, primal and dual local residuals r_n^{k+1}=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1}\\right)^2 (8a); s_n^{k+1}=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}-p_{nm}^{k}\\right)^2 (8b); Parameters \\epsilon^{\\mathrm{pri}} and \\epsilon^{\\mathrm{dual}} denotes primal and dual global feasibility tolerances, respectively."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"ed pressed in € /MWh in the case of an hourly time unit. e. of C. Electrical Distance Cost Allocatio","equation_id":"Eq. (11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To be more precise in how costs are allocated it is possible to use network charges proportional to the electrical distance between agents. As for cab travels, this cost allocation policy would incite agents to trade with their closest electrical partners. Such policy would reflect that long electric distances are costlier to operate due to power losses for example. However, power losses can not directly be considered as they are quadratic, which can not be superposed. When both ends of a trade equally share responsibility and the previous sign convention is followed, network charges become \\gamma_{nm}^{\\mathrm{dist}}=\\pm\\frac{u^{\\mathrm{dist}}d_{nm}}{2}, \\forall(n,m)\\in\\Omega\\times\\omega_n, (11); where d_nm is the electrical distance between agent n and agent m. Distance unit fee u^{\\mathrm{dist}} is expressed in €/MWh/distance unit if the same time unit is used.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"To be more precise in how costs are allocated it is possible to use network charges proportional to the electrical distance between agents. As for cab travels, this cost allocation policy would incite agents to trade with their closest electrical partners. Such policy would reflect that long electric distances are costlier to operate due to power losses for example. However, power losses can not directly be considered as they are quadratic, which can not be superposed. When both ends of a trade equally share responsibility and the previous sign convention is followed, network charges become \\gamma_{nm}^{\\mathrm{dist}}=\\pm\\frac{u^{\\mathrm{dist}}d_{nm}}{2}, \\forall(n,m)\\in\\Omega\\times\\omega_n, (11); where d_nm is the electrical distance between agent n and agent m. Distance unit fee u^{\\mathrm{dist}} is expressed in €/MWh/distance unit if the same time unit is used."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E06","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"ed pressed in € /MWh in the case of an hourly time unit. e. of C. Electrical Distance Cost Allocatio","equation_id":"Eq. (11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To be more precise in how costs are allocated it is possible to use network charges proportional to the electrical distance between agents. As for cab travels, this cost allocation policy would incite agents to trade with their closest electrical partners. Such policy would reflect that long electric distances are costlier to operate due to power losses for example. However, power losses can not directly be considered as they are quadratic, which can not be superposed. When both ends of a trade equally share responsibility and the previous sign convention is followed, network charges become \\gamma^{dist}_{nm} = \\pm \\frac{u^{dist} d_{nm}}{2}, \\quad \\forall (n,m) \\in \\Omega \\times \\omega_n, (11) where dnm is the electrical distance between agent n and agent m. Distance unit fee u^{dist} is expressed in € /MWh/distance unit if the same time unit is used.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"To be more precise in how costs are allocated it is possible to use network charges proportional to the electrical distance between agents. As for cab travels, this cost allocation policy would incite agents to trade with their closest electrical partners. Such policy would reflect that long electric distances are costlier to operate due to power losses for example. However, power losses can not directly be considered as they are quadratic, which can not be superposed. When both ends of a trade equally share responsibility and the previous sign convention is followed, network charges become \\gamma^{dist}_{nm} = \\pm \\frac{u^{dist} d_{nm}}{2}, \\quad \\forall (n,m) \\in \\Omega \\times \\omega_n, (11) where dnm is the electrical distance between agent n and agent m. Distance unit fee u^{dist} is expressed in € /MWh/distance unit if the same time unit is used."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p06-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"opa DC power ﬂow approximation of the entire network, is bet- onter suited for meshed networks such ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"While the unique cost allocation policy does not differentiate agents, the electrical distance one might individualize too much grid tariffs. At the image of Danish public transportation system, using a zonal cost allocation policy seems a good compromise between both. In this situation the electrical network would be divided in several zones associated to distinct zonal unit fees. Each zone could be managed by a different system operator. A zone with a high price would incite outside agents not to trade with agents within, and push within agents towards self-consumption. In this sense, the zonal cost allocation policy allows to economically isolate an area. However, its efficiency strongly depends on zones’ design.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"While the unique cost allocation policy does not differentiate agents, the electrical distance one might individualize too much grid tariffs. At the image of Danish public transportation system, using a zonal cost allocation policy seems a good compromise between both. In this situation the electrical network would be divided in several zones associated to distinct zonal unit fees. Each zone could be managed by a different system operator. A zone with a high price would incite outside agents not to trade with agents within, and push within agents towards self-consumption. In this sense, the zonal cost allocation policy allows to economically isolate an area. However, its efficiency strongly depends on zones’ design."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e23","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"rity dispositions are automated. However, the market as deﬁned pre initially is not intrinsically co","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets \\omega_n could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets \\omega_n could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E02","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"rity dispositions are automated. However, the market as deﬁned pre initially is not intrinsically co","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets ωn could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment.","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","quote":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets ωn could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["price_formation_and_convergence"],"projection_statuses":["composite"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"kind-L6-d9643ebf5c","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L6","ontological_kind":"composite_rule"},"semantic_definition":{},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::coordination.price_signal","paper_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","function":"coordination.price_signal","source_occurrences":[{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R001","market_meaning":"The trading partnership set restricts demand proposals: the bilateral quantity must be zero for a counterparty outside that set. Scope: Buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. Condition: When a prospective counterparty is outside the requesting agent's trading partnership set. Current bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its requested energy quantities. Scope: Buying-side quantity updates in the decentralized consensus-ADMM negotiation of (6). Condition: At each negotiation iteration. Participants update requested quantities through their own optimization (6a) and individually send the resulting trade proposals to their trading partners; the negotiation process repeats until convergence. Scope: Submission of buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. Condition: During each iteration of the negotiation process, until convergence. Requested quantities must be updated consistently with the agent's total-power bounds, with total power equal to the sum of its bilateral proposals. Scope: Requested-power updates in (6a), jointly with the agent's other bilateral proposals. Condition: At every negotiation iteration. Each prosumer's requested bilateral quantity must remain within that prosumer's power boundaries. Scope: Individual buying-side bilateral proposals in the update (6a). Condition: When the requesting agent is a prosumer. The prescribed update prohibits buying requests from generators by constraining their bilateral proposed quantities to be nonnegative. Scope: Bilateral quantity proposals produced by the update (6a). Condition: When the updating agent is a generator.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"Bilateral quantity proposals produced by the update (6a). | Buying-side quantity updates in the decentralized consensus-ADMM negotiation of (6). | Buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. | Individual buying-side bilateral proposals in the update (6a). | Requested-power updates in (6a), jointly with the agent's other bilateral proposals. | Submission of buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002"],"decision_anchor":"bidding.demand","source_rule_sha256":"dd9010023e8d5e8a6fc38f03180aa098dab283f108a8e7f7d06a7719cdb0d88c"},{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"The partner's previous requested quantity enters the averaging and penalty terms used to update the offered quantity. Scope: Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). Condition: The partner counterproposal represents requested power. The previous bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its offered quantities, economically encouraging consensus with its partners. Scope: Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). The trading partnership set restricts possible supply offers: the bilateral quantity to a counterparty outside that set must be zero. Scope: Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n's trading partnership set. The participant sends its updated offered quantities to the corresponding trading partners. Scope: Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration. Condition: After the participant has updated its trade proposals through its own optimization (6a). When updating offered quantities, the participant's total power, summed across all its bilateral proposals, must remain within its lower and upper power bounds. Scope: Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. Each bilateral offered quantity must also lie within the prosumer's individual power boundaries. Scope: Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the prosumer set. Consumers cannot submit positive selling quantities: their bilateral proposal quantities must be nonpositive. Scope: Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the consumer set.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). | Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). | Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). | Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). | Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. | Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. | Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002"],"decision_anchor":"bidding.supply","source_rule_sha256":"eb3e04d94a468918abeb09de0a73b5857fe82afe93cb43afba9b3825477aa259"},{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"Network-charge price incentives, adjusted through unit fees, influence the aggregate-cost-minimizing P2P economic dispatch. Scope: Deterministic, single-time-step exogenous P2P economic dispatch (5). Condition: When the proposed exogenous network-charge policies are applied. The reference clearing objective minimizes the sum of agents' cost functions. Scope: Endogenous, network-constrained P2P economic-dispatch reference formulation (1). Condition: For the reference formulation before replacement of network constraints by exogenous cost allocation.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Deterministic, single-time-step exogenous P2P economic dispatch (5). | Endogenous, network-constrained P2P economic-dispatch reference formulation (1).","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e07","v141-b-g01-e14","v141-b-g03-e11","v141-b-g03-e15"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.social_welfare","source_rule_sha256":"739c152a19529f8dbc08a6ecf72068fc6345650543ebce4f413fa8a328e86477"},{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","market_meaning":"An offered energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals offering energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals. A requested energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals requesting energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals. Network-charge signals are provided transparently before negotiations so participants can anticipate additional costs of using the power system. Scope: Exogenous network-charge signals in the P2P negotiation mechanism. Condition: When exogenous terms are used as network charges. Unit-fee signals may be adapted to grid status, including differences between day and night. Scope: Electrical-distance and zonal network-charge policies, whose underlying grid characteristics are assumed time independent. The system operator may update unit-fee signals based on the previous period's revenue adequacy and congestion occurrence rate; yearly revision is an example, not a mandatory frequency. Scope: Periodic revision of network-charge unit-fee signals under the proposed cost-allocation policies. Condition: When the system operator periodically revises unit fees using historical information from the previous period. Each agent broadcasts its local residuals and tests both global primal and dual stopping criteria; the decentralized negotiation repeats until convergence. Scope: Convergence checks in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: At each iteration, the global stopping test follows receipt of all other agents' local residuals.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). | Convergence checks in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). | Electrical-distance and zonal network-charge policies, whose underlying grid characteristics are assumed time independent. | Exogenous network-charge signals in the P2P negotiation mechanism. | Periodic revision of network-charge unit-fee signals under the proposed cost-allocation policies.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e16","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e19","v141-b-g01-e20","v141-b-g02-E14"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C005","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C006"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.price_signal","source_rule_sha256":"76fd9752e5725e328e480f37d56f7add84aa4d6520433ed0697820cc4a9816e5"},{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R005","market_meaning":"The electrical-distance network-charge signal is intended to encourage agents to trade with their electrically closest partners. Scope: Proposed electrical-distance cost allocation in the P2P community. Condition: Network charges are proportional to the electrical distance between agents. A high zonal price is intended to discourage outside agents from trading with counterparties inside that zone. Scope: Proposed zonal cost allocation with distinct zonal unit fees. Condition: A zone has a high price. Network-charge signals can induce agents to switch from their usual partners to partners unaffected by the malfunctions while retaining the same negotiation routine. Scope: Proposed use of new unit fees to handle deteriorated network operation in the P2P market. Condition: An electric-network incident causes malfunctions affecting trading partners. The bilateral trade power from agent n to agent m must be zero when m is outside n’s partnership set. Scope: Bilateral trading partnerships in the P2P formulation. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n’s trading partnership set. Partnership sets specify possible trades and do not themselves enforce market participation. Scope: Role of partnership sets in the exogenous P2P formulation. Partnership sets could be dynamically adapted in response to congestion; this is a proposed possibility rather than an asserted implemented procedure. Scope: Discussed alternative for handling congestion through changes to partnership sets. Condition: Congestion occurs.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Bilateral trading partnerships in the P2P formulation. | Discussed alternative for handling congestion through changes to partnership sets. | Proposed electrical-distance cost allocation in the P2P community. | Proposed use of new unit fees to handle deteriorated network operation in the P2P market. | Proposed zonal cost allocation with distinct zonal unit fees. | Role of partnership sets in the exogenous P2P formulation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e15","v141-b-g01-e23","v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E02","v141-b-g02-E06","v141-b-g02-E11"]},"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003"],"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","source_rule_sha256":"17c2f49b0636db1a4fd2c1e6da9d2ec09881219b8198137ad05850f17c990419"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Current bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its requested energy quantities. Scope: Buying-side quantity updates in the decentralized consensus-ADMM negotiation of (6). Condition: At each negotiation iteration.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","bidding.demand"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R001::v141-c-R001-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R001","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"a competitive equilibrium which efﬁciency strongly depends on w the chosen network charges. Accordin","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The previous bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its offered quantities, economically encouraging consensus with its partners. Scope: Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6).","expression":"P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n}\\; f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right]","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","bidding.supply"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R002-x-P-n","v141-c-R002-x-p-nm","v141-c-R002-x-p-n"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R002-theta-omega-n","v141-c-R002-theta-gamma-nm","v141-c-R002-theta-gamma-n0","v141-c-R002-theta-lambda-nm-k","v141-c-R002-theta-p-mn-k","v141-c-R002-theta-rho","v141-c-R002-theta-f-n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"a competitive equilibrium which efﬁciency strongly depends on w the chosen network charges. Accordin","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-validation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 4, JULY 2019 fn to be closed, proper, and convex is","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"where penalty factor \\rho>0. Element \\lambda_{nm} of matrix \\Lambda corresponds to generation price of electricity for traded volume p_{nm}. Possible trades of agents n can be grouped in variable P_n=(p_{nm})_{m\\in\\omega_n}. According to [11], supposing cost functions f_n to be closed, proper, and convex is a sufficient condition to ensure convergence of (6). This formulation allows to have primal feasibility of constraints (5c)–(5g) at each iteration step. However, primal feasibility of trades reciprocity (5b) is only verified at the limit after convergence. Note that the additional terms of the augmented Lagrangian in (6a) aims at encouraging, economically, an agent n to reach power consensus with its partners."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Network-charge price incentives, adjusted through unit fees, influence the aggregate-cost-minimizing P2P economic dispatch. Scope: Deterministic, single-time-step exogenous P2P economic dispatch (5). Condition: When the proposed exogenous network-charge policies are applied.","expression":"\\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega,W}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left[f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right]","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-X001","v141-c-R003-X002","v141-c-R003-X003","v141-c-R003-X004"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-T001","v141-c-R003-T002","v141-c-R003-T003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e14","v141-b-g03-e11","v141-b-g03-e15","v141-b-g01-e13"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"for its use of the power system. In problem (3) with (4), reciprocity constraint (3b) is the of only","equation_id":"Eq. (5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In problem (3) with (4), reciprocity constraint (3b) is the only barrier to fully distribute it. To overcome this an additional slack variable W can be considered. Variable W, which can contribute to reach consensus, aims at being the image of all possible trades P. For this, reciprocity constraint (3b) is replaced by power consensus constraint (5b) leading to the deterministic, single time-step, exogenous P2P economic dispatch \\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega,W}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left[f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right] (5a); s.t. (W-W^{T})/2=P (5b); p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}, n\\in\\Omega (5c); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega (5d); p_{nm}\\ge0, n\\in\\Omega_g (5e); p_{nm}\\le0, n\\in\\Omega_c (5f); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega_p. (5g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p09-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Case Studies","anchor_text":"p y , y q allocation, electrical distance and zonal cost allocations enable ma to isolate zones econ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Despite these results, market aggregation by zone does not reflect how the electrical grid is used. Cost allocation policies provides a tool for the system operator to affect the economic dispatch. Unit fees enable to tweak the expected outcomes of the P2P market in such way that line capacities might not be violated. They also offer to the system operator the chance to look for cost recovery."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e15","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p10-c2r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Case Studies","anchor_text":"nomic dispatch, the proposed method based on network charges brings transparency and is simple to im","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Peer-to-peer markets are considered as a likely evolution of the power systems driven by distributed energy resources and ICT development. In this paper a peer-to-peer electricity market including network charges has been considered. Network charges, provided a priori, have been used as incentives to account for grid-related costs in a simple and transparent way. This mechanism incites market participants to respect power system’s limits, rather than enforcing them. Tested for three incentive frameworks, on a novel test case based on the IEEE 39-bus test system, it has been shown the ability of this mechanism to limit the stress put on the physical grid by the market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e13","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"t s pu pose. e , egu a at o u ct o h eva uat g et 2) work constraints is be replaced by a cost alloc","equation_id":"Eq. (4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"However, even though (3) enables the system operator to recover congestion related costs, it does not guarantee the recovery of the costs of maintenance, modernization of power lines, taxes, and policies. This paper proposes to replace the regularization function h with exogenous terms. These exogenous terms would aim not only at allocating congestion-related costs but also costs of maintenance and modernization of power lines, taxes, and policies such as e.g. renewable support schemes. Preference prices as introduced in [18] seems a good candidate for this purpose. Then, regularization function h evaluating network constraints is be replaced by a cost allocation function defined as \\Gamma(\\mathbf{P})=\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left(\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right) (4); where parameter \\gamma_{nm} is the network charge associated to power trade p_{nm} for the given time step. Constant terms \\gamma_n^0, which do not affect the minimization outcome, allow reflecting costs that are independent of the power traded, such as power line investment and maintenance. Network charges \\gamma_{nm}, detailed in Section III, would then account for congestion-related costs and taxes. Function \\Gamma is separable among participants, and will be integrated in their objective function as it will be further discussed in Section III. Note that \\Gamma also represents the amount of money collected by the system operator from community \\Omega for its use of the power system."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"The exogenous network-charge component added to the clearing objective is the cost allocation function Γ(P), the amount collected by the system operator from community Ω for use of the power system.","expression":"\\Gamma(\\mathbf{P})=\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left(\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-X001","v141-c-R003-X003"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-T002","v141-c-R003-T003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e13","v141-b-g01-e14","v141-b-g01-e01"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e13","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"t s pu pose. e , egu a at o u ct o h eva uat g et 2) work constraints is be replaced by a cost alloc","equation_id":"Eq. (4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"However, even though (3) enables the system operator to recover congestion related costs, it does not guarantee the recovery of the costs of maintenance, modernization of power lines, taxes, and policies. This paper proposes to replace the regularization function h with exogenous terms. These exogenous terms would aim not only at allocating congestion-related costs but also costs of maintenance and modernization of power lines, taxes, and policies such as e.g. renewable support schemes. Preference prices as introduced in [18] seems a good candidate for this purpose. Then, regularization function h evaluating network constraints is be replaced by a cost allocation function defined as \\Gamma(\\mathbf{P})=\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left(\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right) (4); where parameter \\gamma_{nm} is the network charge associated to power trade p_{nm} for the given time step. Constant terms \\gamma_n^0, which do not affect the minimization outcome, allow reflecting costs that are independent of the power traded, such as power line investment and maintenance. Network charges \\gamma_{nm}, detailed in Section III, would then account for congestion-related costs and taxes. Function \\Gamma is separable among participants, and will be integrated in their objective function as it will be further discussed in Section III. Note that \\Gamma also represents the amount of money collected by the system operator from community \\Omega for its use of the power system."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"for its use of the power system. In problem (3) with (4), reciprocity constraint (3b) is the of only","equation_id":"Eq. (5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In problem (3) with (4), reciprocity constraint (3b) is the only barrier to fully distribute it. To overcome this an additional slack variable W can be considered. Variable W, which can contribute to reach consensus, aims at being the image of all possible trades P. For this, reciprocity constraint (3b) is replaced by power consensus constraint (5b) leading to the deterministic, single time-step, exogenous P2P economic dispatch \\min_{P,p_n\\in\\Omega,W}\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left[f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right] (5a); s.t. (W-W^{T})/2=P (5b); p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}, n\\in\\Omega (5c); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega (5d); p_{nm}\\ge0, n\\in\\Omega_g (5e); p_{nm}\\le0, n\\in\\Omega_c (5f); \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n, n\\in\\Omega_p. (5g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p01-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"p ( ), wi NOMENCLATURE sto to Λ Matrix of trade prices λnm of agent n with agent m con ·T Matrix tra","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Λ Matrix of trade prices λ_nm of agent n with agent m; \\cdot^{T} Matrix transpose operator; γ_nm Network charges of agent n’s trade with agent m; L Set of lines in the power system; N Set of nodes in the power system; | · | Absolute value operator; Ω Peer-to-peer market community of N_Ω agents; Ω_c Set of consumers (subset of Ω); Ω_g Set of generators (subset of Ω); Ω_p Set of prosumers (subset of Ω); ω_n Set of partners of agent n; P Matrix of trade powers p_nm of agent n with agent m; θ_i Voltage angle at node i; \\underline{p}_n/\\overline{p}_n Lower/upper power boundary of agent n; d_nm Electrical distance between agent n and agent m; f_n Cost function of agent n; N^{zone}_{nm} Number of zones between agent n and agent m; p_n Total amount of power traded by agent n; u Network fee of cost allocation policy; Z_il/Y_il Element of bus impedance/admittance matrix from node i to node l."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"An offered energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals offering energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals.","expression":"\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^{k}-\\rho\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1}\\right)/2","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.supply","coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R004-x-lambda-nm","v141-c-R004-x-p-nm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R004-theta-rho"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20","v141-b-g01-e18"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (1e)-(1g)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In this situation agents can buy a large amount of energy at a low price from one agent to sell it back at a higher price to another. This possibility of arbitraging can be proscribed by limiting the possible amounts traded. Generators, for which p_n\\ge0 and grouped in \\Omega_g, are forbidden to buy power in (1e). On the other hand consumers, for which p_n\\le0 and grouped in \\Omega_c, are forbidden to sell power in (1f). However, prosumers, gathered in \\Omega_p, must still be able to either buy or sell power since they are such that \\underline{p}_n\u003c0\u003c\\overline{p}_n. Power trades of prosumers are bounded by their power boundaries as in (1g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"a competitive equilibrium which efﬁciency strongly depends on w the chosen network charges. Accordin","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-validation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 4, JULY 2019 fn to be closed, proper, and convex is","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"where penalty factor \\rho>0. Element \\lambda_{nm} of matrix \\Lambda corresponds to generation price of electricity for traded volume p_{nm}. Possible trades of agents n can be grouped in variable P_n=(p_{nm})_{m\\in\\omega_n}. According to [11], supposing cost functions f_n to be closed, proper, and convex is a sufficient condition to ensure convergence of (6). This formulation allows to have primal feasibility of constraints (5c)–(5g) at each iteration step. However, primal feasibility of trades reciprocity (5b) is only verified at the limit after convergence. Note that the additional terms of the augmented Lagrangian in (6a) aims at encouraging, economically, an agent n to reach power consensus with its partners."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"A requested energy quantity in a partner's counterproposal supplies an input to the receiving agent's bilateral trading-price update. Scope: Bilateral counterproposal exchange in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: For received counterproposals requesting energy; the receiving agent has received all partner counterproposals.","expression":"\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^{k}-\\rho\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1}\\right)/2","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R004-x-lambda-nm","v141-c-R004-x-p-nm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R004-theta-rho"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20","v141-b-g01-e18"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (1e)-(1g)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In this situation agents can buy a large amount of energy at a low price from one agent to sell it back at a higher price to another. This possibility of arbitraging can be proscribed by limiting the possible amounts traded. Generators, for which p_n\\ge0 and grouped in \\Omega_g, are forbidden to buy power in (1e). On the other hand consumers, for which p_n\\le0 and grouped in \\Omega_c, are forbidden to sell power in (1f). However, prosumers, gathered in \\Omega_p, must still be able to either buy or sell power since they are such that \\underline{p}_n\u003c0\u003c\\overline{p}_n. Power trades of prosumers are bounded by their power boundaries as in (1g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"a competitive equilibrium which efﬁciency strongly depends on w the chosen network charges. Accordin","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-validation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 4, JULY 2019 fn to be closed, proper, and convex is","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"where penalty factor \\rho>0. Element \\lambda_{nm} of matrix \\Lambda corresponds to generation price of electricity for traded volume p_{nm}. Possible trades of agents n can be grouped in variable P_n=(p_{nm})_{m\\in\\omega_n}. According to [11], supposing cost functions f_n to be closed, proper, and convex is a sufficient condition to ensure convergence of (6). This formulation allows to have primal feasibility of constraints (5c)–(5g) at each iteration step. However, primal feasibility of trades reciprocity (5b) is only verified at the limit after convergence. Note that the additional terms of the augmented Lagrangian in (6a) aims at encouraging, economically, an agent n to reach power consensus with its partners."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Network-charge signals are provided transparently before negotiations so participants can anticipate additional costs of using the power system. Scope: Exogenous network-charge signals in the P2P negotiation mechanism. Condition: When exogenous terms are used as network charges.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e16","v141-b-g01-e04","v141-b-g01-e21"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e16","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"Note that the deﬁnition of partnership set ωn only inform on agent n’s possible trades but does not ","equation_id":"Eq. (5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"As proven in Appendix A, when no network charges are applied, i.e. all \\gamma_{nm}=0, trading prices \\lambda_{nm} are uniform and equal to the pool market price. The system operator does not intervene in solving (5) as it only provides network charges \\gamma_{nm} a priori. Hence, network charges are provided in a transparent manner before negotiations such that agents can anticipate on the over costs brought by the use of the power system."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p02-c1r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"gy g , pp y a network charge component. This network charge component is not only to recover all net","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In contrast, the bilateral contracts considered here have a component that is based on energy generation costs, supplemented by a network charge component. This network charge component is not only to recover all network costs but also other costs e.g. operational, taxes and policy-related costs. Hence, network constraints are not forced directly but rather accommodated through these network charges. Another important benefit of this approach is that market participants have knowledge of network charges prior to the negotiation process. Contrary to a classical economic dispatch, this transparency on network charges enables agents to anticipate on what it will cost them to trade on the network."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e21","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"local residuals. In this context it would be interesting to use a secured mechanism, as does [15] fo","equation_id":"Eq. (4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"When the goal is to obtain a P2P market with allocation of grid-related costs it is possible to use network charges as in (4). Contrary to preference prices chosen by agents, parameters \\gamma_{nm} are provided by the system operator a priori when used as network charges. As mentioned in Section I, remember that, in this paper, a cost allocation policy refers to the way costs are divided between P2P market participants. The proposed cost allocation policies will define how network charges are estimated."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Unit-fee signals may be adapted to grid status, including differences between day and night. Scope: Electrical-distance and zonal network-charge policies, whose underlying grid characteristics are assumed time independent.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E14"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E14","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"operator can periodically, e.g. yearly, update unit fees based et- on the revenue adequacy and the c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Both electrical distance and zonal cost allocation policies depend on grid characteristics, supposed time independent. Their unit fees can be adapted to grid’s status (e.g. between day and night). As any exogenous approach the proposed allocation policies may not ensure efficiency of the P2P market, as pointed in [16] in the case of transmission rights. Even though local marginal prices seem effective, they may be largely rejected for their opacity by P2P market participants anxious for transparency. To define the unit fees, as well as zones, the system operator can periodically, e.g. yearly, update unit fees based on the revenue adequacy and the congestion occurrence rate of the last period. This type of historical data analysis is a common practice. For example the French transmission operator, RTE, publishes its transmission tariffs (or TURPE for Tarif d’Utilisation du Réseau Public d’Electricité) based on this type of analysis. More details on this method can be found in the “Study on tariff design for distribution systems” prepared for the European Commission. Alternatively, zones can follow administrative delimitation such as states."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The system operator may update unit-fee signals based on the previous period's revenue adequacy and congestion occurrence rate; yearly revision is an example, not a mandatory frequency. Scope: Periodic revision of network-charge unit-fee signals under the proposed cost-allocation policies. Condition: When the system operator periodically revises unit fees using historical information from the previous period.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E14"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E14","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"operator can periodically, e.g. yearly, update unit fees based et- on the revenue adequacy and the c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Both electrical distance and zonal cost allocation policies depend on grid characteristics, supposed time independent. Their unit fees can be adapted to grid’s status (e.g. between day and night). As any exogenous approach the proposed allocation policies may not ensure efficiency of the P2P market, as pointed in [16] in the case of transmission rights. Even though local marginal prices seem effective, they may be largely rejected for their opacity by P2P market participants anxious for transparency. To define the unit fees, as well as zones, the system operator can periodically, e.g. yearly, update unit fees based on the revenue adequacy and the congestion occurrence rate of the last period. This type of historical data analysis is a common practice. For example the French transmission operator, RTE, publishes its transmission tariffs (or TURPE for Tarif d’Utilisation du Réseau Public d’Electricité) based on this type of analysis. More details on this method can be found in the “Study on tariff design for distribution systems” prepared for the European Commission. Alternatively, zones can follow administrative delimitation such as states."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C006","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Each agent broadcasts its local residuals and tests both global primal and dual stopping criteria; the decentralized negotiation repeats until convergence. Scope: Convergence checks in the decentralized P2P negotiation (6). Condition: At each iteration, the global stopping test follows receipt of all other agents' local residuals.","expression":"\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left\\|r_n^{k+1}\\right\\|^2\\le\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{pri}2},\\quad \\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left\\|s_n^{k+1}\\right\\|^2\\le\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{dual}2};\\quad r_n^{k+1}=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1}\\right)^2,\\quad s_n^{k+1}=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}-p_{nm}^{k}\\right)^2","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R004-x-p-nm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R004-theta-epsilon-pri","v141-c-R004-theta-epsilon-dual"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e19","v141-b-g01-e20"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C006","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e19","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-validation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"rm on. n∈Ω n∈Ω the with, respectively, primal and dual local residuals ies 2 rk+1n = pk+1nm + pk+1mn","equation_id":"Eq. (7)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Global stopping criteria associated to (6) are such as \\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left\\|r_n^{k+1}\\right\\|^2\\le\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{pri}2} and \\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left\\|s_n^{k+1}\\right\\|^2\\le\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{dual}2} (7); with, respectively, primal and dual local residuals r_n^{k+1}=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1}\\right)^2 (8a); s_n^{k+1}=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left(p_{nm}^{k+1}-p_{nm}^{k}\\right)^2 (8b); Parameters \\epsilon^{\\mathrm{pri}} and \\epsilon^{\\mathrm{dual}} denotes primal and dual global feasibility tolerances, respectively."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The electrical-distance network-charge signal is intended to encourage agents to trade with their electrically closest partners. Scope: Proposed electrical-distance cost allocation in the P2P community. Condition: Network charges are proportional to the electrical distance between agents.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e27","v141-b-g02-E06"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"ed pressed in € /MWh in the case of an hourly time unit. e. of C. Electrical Distance Cost Allocatio","equation_id":"Eq. (11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To be more precise in how costs are allocated it is possible to use network charges proportional to the electrical distance between agents. As for cab travels, this cost allocation policy would incite agents to trade with their closest electrical partners. Such policy would reflect that long electric distances are costlier to operate due to power losses for example. However, power losses can not directly be considered as they are quadratic, which can not be superposed. When both ends of a trade equally share responsibility and the previous sign convention is followed, network charges become \\gamma_{nm}^{\\mathrm{dist}}=\\pm\\frac{u^{\\mathrm{dist}}d_{nm}}{2}, \\forall(n,m)\\in\\Omega\\times\\omega_n, (11); where d_nm is the electrical distance between agent n and agent m. Distance unit fee u^{\\mathrm{dist}} is expressed in €/MWh/distance unit if the same time unit is used."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E06","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"ed pressed in € /MWh in the case of an hourly time unit. e. of C. Electrical Distance Cost Allocatio","equation_id":"Eq. (11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To be more precise in how costs are allocated it is possible to use network charges proportional to the electrical distance between agents. As for cab travels, this cost allocation policy would incite agents to trade with their closest electrical partners. Such policy would reflect that long electric distances are costlier to operate due to power losses for example. However, power losses can not directly be considered as they are quadratic, which can not be superposed. When both ends of a trade equally share responsibility and the previous sign convention is followed, network charges become \\gamma^{dist}_{nm} = \\pm \\frac{u^{dist} d_{nm}}{2}, \\quad \\forall (n,m) \\in \\Omega \\times \\omega_n, (11) where dnm is the electrical distance between agent n and agent m. Distance unit fee u^{dist} is expressed in € /MWh/distance unit if the same time unit is used."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"A high zonal price is intended to discourage outside agents from trading with counterparties inside that zone. Scope: Proposed zonal cost allocation with distinct zonal unit fees. Condition: A zone has a high price.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E11"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p06-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"opa DC power ﬂow approximation of the entire network, is bet- onter suited for meshed networks such ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"While the unique cost allocation policy does not differentiate agents, the electrical distance one might individualize too much grid tariffs. At the image of Danish public transportation system, using a zonal cost allocation policy seems a good compromise between both. In this situation the electrical network would be divided in several zones associated to distinct zonal unit fees. Each zone could be managed by a different system operator. A zone with a high price would incite outside agents not to trade with agents within, and push within agents towards self-consumption. In this sense, the zonal cost allocation policy allows to economically isolate an area. However, its efficiency strongly depends on zones’ design."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R005-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Network-charge signals can induce agents to switch from their usual partners to partners unaffected by the malfunctions while retaining the same negotiation routine. Scope: Proposed use of new unit fees to handle deteriorated network operation in the P2P market. Condition: An electric-network incident causes malfunctions affecting trading partners.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e23","v141-b-g02-E02"],"ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R005::v141-c-R005-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R005","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e23","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"rity dispositions are automated. However, the market as deﬁned pre initially is not intrinsically co","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets \\omega_n could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E02","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"rity dispositions are automated. However, the market as deﬁned pre initially is not intrinsically co","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Finally, when an incident occurs on the electric network security dispositions are automated. However, the market as defined initially is not intrinsically considering this deteriorated mode. Partnership sets ωn could be dynamically adapted in case of congestion. But this would require to duplicate the number of signals sent by the system operator to market participants. In addition, participants would have to manage different routines. A simpler way to influence agents is to apply new unit fees. That way network charges offer an indirect mechanism to handle deteriorated modes. Thus, network charges can push agents to shift from their usual partners to others unaffected by the malfunctions while keeping the same routine. At the limit, this operating mode of cost allocation policies enables market islanding. This corresponds to a security market procedure with the least grid stress while waiting for repairment."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R002-x-p-nm","symbol":"p_{nm}","meaning":"Bilateral power quantity from agent n to agent m in the local offered-quantity update; its sign is restricted by agent role in Eq. (6a), and it is zero for m outside n's partnership set in Eq. (2).","kind":"operational","domain":"real-valued bilateral power quantity over m in omega_n, with p_{nm}=0 for m not in omega_n (Eq. 2)","unit":null,"indices":["n","m"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::x::v141-c-R002-x-p-nm"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R002-x-P-n","symbol":"P_n","meaning":"Vector of agent n's possible bilateral trade proposals, P_n=(p_{nm})_{m in omega_n}, chosen in the local optimization (6a).","kind":"operational","domain":"collection of real-valued bilateral quantities p_{nm} over partners m in omega_n","unit":null,"indices":["n","k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::x::v141-c-R002-x-P-n"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R002-x-p-n","symbol":"p_n","meaning":"Total amount of power traded by agent n, constrained to equal the sum of its bilateral proposals in the local update (6a).","kind":"operational","domain":"real-valued total traded power linked by p_n=sum_{m in omega_n} p_{nm} and bounded by the agent's power boundaries","unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::x::v141-c-R002-x-p-n"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X001","symbol":"P","meaning":"Matrix of bilateral trade powers p_nm of agent n with agent m, used as an optimization variable in the clearing formulations.","kind":"operational","domain":"real matrix of bilateral trade powers over the market community; source does not give a numeric unit in the quoted definition","unit":null,"indices":["n","m"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e14"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-X001"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X002","symbol":"p_n","meaning":"Total amount of power traded by agent n.","kind":"operational","domain":"real scalar; source states positive when selling and negative when buying","unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-X002"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X003","symbol":"p_nm","meaning":"Bilateral trade power of agent n with agent m.","kind":"operational","domain":"real scalar entry of P; source states it is necessarily zero when m is not in n's trading partnership set","unit":null,"indices":["n","m"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e09"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-X003"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X004","symbol":"W","meaning":"Auxiliary matrix variable used in the power-consensus constraint; the source describes it as the image of all possible trades P.","kind":"operational","domain":"real matrix with entries w_nm, used with the consensus relation (W-W^T)/2=P","unit":null,"indices":["n","m"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e14"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-X004"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R004-x-lambda-nm","symbol":"\\lambda_{nm}^{k}","meaning":"Bilateral trading price, described as the generation price of electricity for traded volume p_{nm}, at negotiation iteration k; it is updated from received counterproposal quantities.","kind":"operational","domain":"Not explicitly specified in the supplied source; occurs as a price variable in the algebraic update Eq. (6b).","unit":null,"indices":["n\\in\\Omega","m\\in\\omega_n","k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e11","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::x::v141-c-R004-x-lambda-nm"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R004-x-p-nm","symbol":"p_{nm}^{k}","meaning":"Signed bilateral traded power from agent n to agent m at negotiation iteration k; positive when agent n is selling and negative when buying.","kind":"operational","domain":"Signed real power with role-dependent restrictions in Eq. (6a): p_{nm}\\ge0 if n\\in\\Omega_g, p_{nm}\\le0 if n\\in\\Omega_c, and \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n if n\\in\\Omega_p.","unit":null,"indices":["n\\in\\Omega","m\\in\\omega_n","k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::x::v141-c-R004-x-p-nm"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-omega-n","symbol":"\\omega_n","meaning":"Set of partners of agent n, i.e. agent n's trading partnership set.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"set of partners of agent n"},"unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e20"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::theta::v141-c-R002-theta-omega-n"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-gamma-nm","symbol":"\\gamma_{nm}","meaning":"Network charge associated with power trade p_{nm} for the given time step, provided a priori by the system operator as an exogenous charge.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"given a priori network charge"},"unit":null,"indices":["n","m"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e13","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e21","v141-b-g01-e24"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::theta::v141-c-R002-theta-gamma-nm"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-gamma-n0","symbol":"\\gamma_n^0","meaning":"Constant network-charge term that does not depend on the power traded and does not affect the minimization outcome.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"given constant network-charge term"},"unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e13","v141-b-g01-e17"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::theta::v141-c-R002-theta-gamma-n0"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-lambda-nm-k","symbol":"\\lambda_{nm}^{k}","meaning":"Bilateral trade-price signal from iteration k for traded volume p_{nm}; it enters agent n's local offered-quantity update in (6a).","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"iteration-k trade-price signal"},"unit":null,"indices":["n","m","k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::theta::v141-c-R002-theta-lambda-nm-k"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-rho","symbol":"\\rho","meaning":"Positive penalty factor in the consensus-ADMM local objective (6a).","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::theta::v141-c-R002-theta-rho"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-f-n","symbol":"f_n","meaning":"Cost function of agent n in the local objective; the source states closed, proper, and convex f_n is sufficient for convergence of (6).","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"given cost function of agent n"},"unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::theta::v141-c-R002-theta-f-n"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-p-mn-k","symbol":"p_{mn}^{k}","meaning":"Partner m's previous bilateral quantity/counterproposal at iteration k that enters agent n's averaging and penalty terms in (6a).","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"iteration-k partner counterproposal quantity"},"unit":null,"indices":["m","n","k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::theta::v141-c-R002-theta-p-mn-k"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-T001","symbol":"f_n","meaning":"Cost function of agent n.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"agent-specific cost function f_n"},"unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e07"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::theta::v141-c-R003-T001"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-T002","symbol":"\\gamma_{nm}","meaning":"Network charge associated with agent n's trade with agent m for the given time step; it is an exogenous term in the network-charge objective.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"gamma_nm"},"unit":null,"indices":["n","m"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e13","v141-b-g01-e14"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::theta::v141-c-R003-T002"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-T003","symbol":"\\gamma_n^0","meaning":"Constant network-charge term for agent n that does not depend on the power traded.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"gamma_n^0"},"unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e13","v141-b-g01-e14"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R003::theta::v141-c-R003-T003"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R004-theta-rho","symbol":"\\rho","meaning":"Penalty factor in the consensus-ADMM coordination update; the source states \\rho>0.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":">0"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::theta::v141-c-R004-theta-rho"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R004-theta-epsilon-pri","symbol":"\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{pri}}","meaning":"Primal global feasibility tolerance in the stopping criterion associated with the decentralized negotiation.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e19"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::theta::v141-c-R004-theta-epsilon-pri"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R004-theta-epsilon-dual","symbol":"\\epsilon^{\\mathrm{dual}}","meaning":"Dual global feasibility tolerance in the stopping criterion associated with the decentralized negotiation.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e19"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::theta::v141-c-R004-theta-epsilon-dual"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::coordination.price_signal","stage":"L6","subtype":"price_formation_and_convergence","projection_status":"composite","target_definition":"The bilateral transaction price is updated according to λ_nm^{k+1}=λ_nm^k−ρ(p_nm^{k+1}+p_mn^{k+1})/2. The system operator announces exogenous network charges ex ante and may revise the unit rate according to network conditions or historical revenue adequacy and congestion conditions. The unit also independently contains residual broadcasting and global primal and dual stopping procedures.","form_kind":"procedural","identity_fields":["Object: the bilateral transaction price, the exogenous network-charge signal, and the negotiation convergence criterion","Core operation: updating the transaction price according to quantity inconsistency between the two parties; announcing and revising the rate; aggregating residuals for the stopping decision","Output: the updated price and charge signals, and the judgment to continue negotiation or to stop"],"variation_fields":["The transaction price is updated after counterproposals from all partners have been received, ρ>0","The grid features of electrical distance and zoning policy are assumed not to change with time; the unit rate can adapt to daytime and nighttime network conditions","Periodic revision is based on the previous period's revenue adequacy and congestion incidence; annual revision is only an example","The residual test and the global primal and dual tolerance tests are retained respectively according to source equations (7)–(8); stopping occurs only when both criteria are satisfied","The price entering local optimization, the clearing objective, and partner selection belong to the use context of the signal"],"constraint_refs":["17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C005","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C006"],"paper_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","function":"coordination.price_signal","template_code":"BCT"}],"source_variants":[],"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/20"});
